    - { name: "Cryptography", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { choose: 2, from: ["Literacy", "Literacy: Native Language", "Literacy: Other"], bonus: 20, note: "Literacy in two languages (+20%)." }
    - { name: "Computer Operation", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Computer Hacking", bonus: 5, note: "+5% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Surveillance", bonus: 10, note: "Printed as Surveillance Systems (+10%)." }
    - { name: "Intelligence", bonus: 15, note: "+15% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Disguise", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Forgery", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "Prowl", bonus: 10, note: "+10% bonus over base." }
    - { name: "W.P. Energy Pistol", base: 0, per_level: 0 }
    - { choose: 2, categories: ["Weapon Proficiencies"], note: "W.P.: two of choice." }
    - { name: "Hand to Hand: Expert", base: 0, per_level: 0, note: "May be changed to Martial Arts at the cost of one other skill, or Assassin if of an evil alignment." }
  occ_related_skills:
    count: 8
    categories:
      - { name: "Communications", bonus: 5 }
      - "Domestic"
      - "Electrical"
      - { name: "Espionage", bonus: 10 }
      - "Mechanical"
      - { name: "Medical", only: ["First Aid", "Paramedic"] }
      - { name: "Military", bonus: 10 }
      - "Physical"
      - { name: "Pilot", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Pilot Related", bonus: 5 }
      - { name: "Rogue", bonus: 10 }
      - "Science"
      - { name: "Technical", bonus: 5 }
      - "Weapon Proficiencies"
      - "Wilderness"
    schedule: [{ level: 3, count: 2 }, { level: 6, count: 2 }, { level: 9, count: 1 }, { level: 12, count: 1 }]
    note: "Select eight other skills, plus two more at level three, two at level six and one each at levels nine and twelve. AT LEAST TWO OF THE EIGHT MUST COME FROM ESPIONAGE AND TWO FROM ROGUE, which is a floor per category and not a limit - `occ_related_skills` has one open count over all the categories it lists, so the requirement is stated here and the player honours it."
  secondary_skills:
    count: 8
    note: "Eight secondary skills from the same list, without the bonuses in parentheses, starting at the base skill level. The any/only limits above still apply."
equipment_starting:
  - { choose: 1, label: "energy pistol", qty: 1, from: ["hi-30-laser-pistol", "hi-10-heavy-laser-pistol", "ne-4-plasma-cartridge-pistol", "ep-5-energy-pulse-pistol", "gr-10p-pistol", "ph-21-phase-beamer"] }
  - { choose: 1, label: "vibro-blade of choice", qty: 1, from: ["vibro-knife", "vibro-saber", "vibro-sword"] }
  - { item_id: "hand-held-computer", qty: 1 }
  - { item_id: "light-combat-armor", qty: 1 }
extraction_notes: |
  - Read from the OCR cache of a scan and verified against 200 dpi renders of
    printed 82 and 83. The heading and the lore are at the foot of printed 82;
    the whole stat block is on 83, split across both its columns, and the render
    was read to settle which of the two category lists on that page belongs to
    this class and which belongs to the Freedom Fighter below it. They differ:
    this class has Espionage +10%, Rogue +10%, Domestic with no bonus and Pilot
    Related +5%, where the Freedom Fighter has +5%, +5%, Domestic +5% and Pilot
    Related +10%. Reading the OCR alone would have been a coin toss.
  - THE TWO-FROM-ESPIONAGE, TWO-FROM-ROGUE FLOOR IS NOT ENFORCED. The book
    requires at least two of the eight related picks from Espionage and two from
    Rogue. `occ_related_skills` carries a single `count` over every category it
    lists; `occ_skills` can express a per-category floor as a choice group, but
    moving four picks there would take them out of the eight the book says are
    free and shrink the choice. Stated in the related-skills note and in the GM
    Notes instead. The Freedom Fighter carries the same rule and the same note.
  - occ_group is `optional` rather than `men-of-arms`, matching the TVIA
    Inspector - this class is the Empire''''s mirror of it. The book gives this
    entry no section grouping of its own; it sits in the Transgalactic Empire
    chapter, which is a nation and not one of the five O.C.C. groups.
  - FOUR EQUIPMENT ITEMS ARE NOT IMPORTED and the book gives stats for none of
    them: SURVEILLANCE EQUIPMENT, a DISGUISE KIT, FAKE PAPERS and a MICRO-CAMERA
    "the size of a pin, can be hidden anywhere". They are the four most
    characteristic things this class carries and there is no catalog row for any
    of them. A stub marked "needs stats" would never be filled, because no
    Palladium book stats them; they are in the GM Notes so a table can hand them
    over. This is the sharpest instance so far of a class shipping without the
    items its own book issues.
  - "Light body armor" is read as this book''''s own Light Combat Armor, printed
    122, rather than left generic. "Energy pistol" and "vibro-blade of choice"
    are choice groups bounded to the catalog - the vibro-blades are the general
    Rifts rows, since this book prints no vibro weapon of its own.
  - THE 1D4 CYBERNETIC IMPLANTS ARE NOT STORED. The book allows this class to
    start with 1D4 of them and there is no field for cybernetics and no catalog
    of implants to draw from. A table decision, and the same gap the Wolfen
    Quatoria recorded.
  - Experience table transcribed from printed 183. The column heads "Freedom
    Fighter & Imperial Security / Galactic Tracer, Runner" and prints the same
    fifteen numbers as the "TVIA Agent, CAF Fleet Officer, Imperial Legionnaire"
    column beside it - checked digit by digit on the render, because two
    identical ladders under different headings reads like a transcription error
    and is not.
  - This class has NO S.D.C. or hit point formula of its own, so the core rule
    reaches it via CORE_SDC_BY_CLASS at 1D6 - not a man of arms, on the same
    reading that made it `optional`.
---

## Lore

The dreaded secret police of the Transgalactic Empire, drawing operatives from
every race. The work is internal security, policing the Empire''s subjects, and
intelligence - information gathering, espionage, sabotage and infiltration.

Imperial Security agents are known for ruthlessness and deviousness. They
usually masquerade as regular Legion officers, or go undercover as civilians,
scientists, businessmen, criminals or freedom fighters.

## GM Notes

Available as a player character with the Game Master''s approval.

Two rules the sheet cannot hold. **At least two of the eight related skill picks
must come from Espionage and two from Rogue** - the app offers all eight freely,
so hold the player to it. And the class **may start with 1D4 cybernetic
implants**, which there is no field for.

Four things this class carries are missing from its equipment list because the
catalog has no row for them and the book gives them no stats: **surveillance
equipment, a disguise kit, fake papers, and a micro-camera the size of a pin
that can be hidden anywhere.** Hand them over at the table. The book also notes
that additional weapons, explosives, armour and equipment may be issued for a
particular assignment.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'imperial-security-agent');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'imperial-security-agent';

